843 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "6452e741-3219-5674-aa9c-11f468eb9f0b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549866 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186549332 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549866 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186549478 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549866 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186549542 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549866 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186549735 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549866 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186549783 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549866 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549866 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549866 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186549478 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549866 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "8bd771f8-1da2-5ff5-b018-e0a61817e437" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549879 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186549395 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549879 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549879 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549879 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186549395 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549879 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "a2a58eb4-92a4-5719-9dfb-a3aad6a40bc0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549882 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186549498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549882 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549882 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549882 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186549498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549882 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "ac508ebb-eb69-5e10-b789-82b08fb0c3a2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549893 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186549400 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549893 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186549436 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549893 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186549518 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549893 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186549664 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549893 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186549668 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549893 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549893 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549893 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186549436 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549893 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "b9a3fd1a-2273-5c72-90b6-7e74178421d4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549950 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186549796 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549950 #edb/ref 1069 "q-215096" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549950 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549950 #edb/ref 1071 "If $v=2τdh,$ then $τ=${{field-1}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549950 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2c796978-9336-5fc7-a4cc-bd1fe1ccd4cd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549950 #edb/ref 1074 "We need to isolate $τ,$ so we will treat the other variables $v,d,$ and $h$ as constants. We can use the multiplication principle to solve for $τ.$\n\nFirst, we rearrange and then divide both sides by $2dh:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =2τdh \\\\ v & =(2dh)τ \\\\ \\frac{v}{2dh} & =\\frac{(2dh)τ}{2dh} \\\\ \\frac{v}{2dh} & =τ\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $τ=\\frac{v}{2dh}.$\n\n**Note:** This result assumes that $d\\ne 0$ and $h\\ne 0.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549961 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186549843 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549961 #edb/ref 1069 "q-103266" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549961 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549961 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/4f/4f538bb715c53ad0478539feaef29c725f6f6e494134726faede38586e60a4d6.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the angles shown above?\n\nI. $m∠AOB=m∠BOC$\n\nII. $m∠BOC=m∠COD$\n\nIII. $∠AOB≅∠DOE$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549961 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "47312f69-f326-5376-9041-14157a2dd5a5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549961 #edb/ref 1074 "From the diagram, we see that \n\n- $∠AOB$ and $∠BOC$ are marked with $|$, and\n\n- $∠COD$ and $∠DOE$ are marked with $||.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∠AOB & ≅∠BOC \\\\ ∠COD & ≅∠DOE.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠AOB & =m∠BOC \\\\ m∠COD & =m∠DOE.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo statement I is true, while statements II and III are false." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549963 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186549817 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549963 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186549882 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549963 #edb/ref 1069 "q-171612" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549963 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549963 #edb/ref 1071 "The solution to the inequality $-2a\\ge 10$ is $a${{field-1}}{{field-2}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549963 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5c6ca484-3f17-5181-8810-930cfb1991ba" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549963 #edb/ref 1074 "The variable $a$ is being *multiplied* by $-2.$ To isolate $a,$ we perform the *opposite* operation, which is *dividing* by $-2.$ Remember to perform the same operation on both sides.\n\nAlso, when multiplying or dividing an inequality by a negative number, we need to flip the inequality sign:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2a\\,\\, & \\ge \\,\\,10 \\\\ \\frac{-2a}{-2}\\,\\, & \\le \\,\\,\\frac{10}{-2} \\\\ \\frac{-2a}{-2}\\,\\, & \\le \\,\\,-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can cancel a common factor of $-2$ from both the numerator and denominator.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{-2a}{-2} & \\le -5 \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{-2}a}{\\cancel{-2}} & \\le -5 \\\\ a & \\le -5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $a\\le -5.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549989 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186549829 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549989 #edb/ref 1069 "q-2765" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549989 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549989 #edb/ref 1071 "Factor the expression $4{r}^{2}+16r+16.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549989 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "af2a1028-5c68-5261-af13-01fa26bc76aa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549989 #edb/ref 1074 "Notice that all of the terms have a common factor of $4.$ Therefore, we can simplify the expression by factoring out $4,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{r}^{2}+16r+16 & = \\\\ 4({r}^{2}+4r+4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's factor the expression inside parentheses using the square of a sum formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{2}+4r+4 & = \\\\ {r}^{2}+4r+{2}^{2} & = \\\\ {r}^{2}+2\\cdot 2r+{2}^{2} & = \\\\ (r+2)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, our original expression factors as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4({r}^{2}+4r+4) & = \\\\ 4(r+2)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549995 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186549866 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549995 #edb/ref 1069 "q-68963" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549995 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549995 #edb/ref 1071 "${({5}^{3}\\times 3)}^{4}=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549995 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c20addaa-2ef3-5691-8ace-1f1d2895fe58" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549995 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we apply the power of product rule for exponents:\n\n$$\n{({5}^{3}\\times 3)}^{4}={({5}^{3})}^{4}\\times {3}^{4}\n$$\n\nThen, we rewrite the first factor using the power rule for exponents, and simplify:\n\n$$\n{({5}^{3})}^{4}\\times {3}^{4}={5}^{3\\cdot 4}\\times {3}^{4}={5}^{12}\\times {3}^{4}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186550003 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186549893 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186550003 #edb/ref 1069 "q-63877" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186550003 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186550003 #edb/ref 1071 "Evaluate $\\sqrt[3]{5y+1}$ at $y=3.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186550003 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d9b9c93d-9a95-57ab-bfc8-b62b1c153ff8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186550003 #edb/ref 1074 "We replace $y$ with $3$ in the given expression, and simplify as much as possible:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[3]{5y+1} & = \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{5\\cdot 3+1} & = \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{15+1} & = \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{16} & = \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{8\\cdot 2} & = \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{8}\\cdot \\sqrt[3]{2} & = \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{{2}^{3}}\\cdot \\sqrt[3]{2} & = \\\\ 2\\sqrt[3]{2} & \\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186550006 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186549832 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186550006 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186549834 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186550006 #edb/ref 1069 "q-215510" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186550006 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186550006 #edb/ref 1071 "Written in ascending order, the solutions to $|1-2x|-2=7$ are $x=${{field-1}}\nand $x=$\n{{field-2}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186550006 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e2cc5506-dc0b-5a5b-8907-9924a3a993c1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186550006 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we isolate the absolute value on the left side of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|1-2x|-2 & =7 \\\\ |1-2x| & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the following two cases:\n\n- If $|1-2x|=1-2x,$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|1-2x| & =9 \\\\ 1-2x & =9 \\\\ -2x & =8 \\\\ x & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- If $|1-2x|=-(1-2x),$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|1-2x| & =9 \\\\ -(1-2x) & =9 \\\\ -1+2x & =9 \\\\ 2x & =10 \\\\ x & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the solutions are $x=-4$ and $x=5.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186550009 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186549879 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186550009 #edb/ref 1069 "q-208694" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186550009 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186550009 #edb/ref 1071 "If $2mn+m+4=7,$ then $m=${{field-1}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186550009 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ea72a94a-bdd5-5d3c-b2b4-c2e3488df948" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186550009 #edb/ref 1074 "We need to isolate all terms with $m$ on one side of the equation. To do this, we subtract $4$ to both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2mn+m+4 & =7 \\\\ 2mn+m+4-4 & =7-4 \\\\ 2mn+m & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we factor out $m$ in the expression on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2mn+m & =3 \\\\ m(2n+1) & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we divide both sides by $(2n+1)$ to isolate $m:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m(2n+1) & =3 \\\\ \\frac{m(2n+1)}{2n+1} & =\\frac{3}{2n+1} \\\\ \\frac{m\\cancel{(2n+1)}}{\\cancel{2n+1}} & =\\frac{3}{2n+1} \\\\ m & =\\frac{3}{2n+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** This result assumes that $2n+1\\ne 0.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186550012 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186549811 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186550012 #edb/ref 1069 "q-117951" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186550012 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186550012 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/30/304c79983ae3d5cdc007d62aa8e30bac247c1370239a9029a33b21b7fcf2f309.png)\n\nOrder the sides of the triangle $△RST$ above from the shortest to the longest." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186550012 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f2983b81-0133-59cc-8912-96155bb43215" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186550012 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let's find the missing angle $T.$ Since the interior angles of a triangle sum to ${180}^{∘},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠R+m∠S+m∠T & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ {40}^{∘}+{103}^{∘}+m∠T & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠T & ={180}^{∘}-{143}^{∘} \\\\ m∠T & ={37}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $∠T$ is the smallest angle, its opposite side $\\overset{―}{RS}$ is the shortest side of the triangle.\n\nSince $∠S$ is the largest angle, its opposite side $\\overset{―}{RT}$ must be the longest side of the triangle. \n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nRS<ST<RT\n$$\n\nand therefore, the required order is\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{RS},\\overset{―}{ST},\\overset{―}{RT}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186550022 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186549803 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186550022 #edb/ref 1069 "q-44680" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186550022 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186550022 #edb/ref 1071 "Evaluate the polynomial $\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{3z}{4}-\\frac{1}{2}$ at $z=2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186550022 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "fe6f82c7-c73d-5fb8-9cbc-ff242831dba6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186550022 #edb/ref 1074 "To evaluate, we substitute $z=2$  into the polynomial and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{3z}{4}-\\frac{1}{2} & = \\\\ \\frac{{2}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{3(2)}{4}-\\frac{1}{2} & = \\\\ \\frac{4}{4}+\\frac{3}{2}-\\frac{1}{2} & = \\\\ 1+1 & = \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186550039 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186550047 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186550039 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2ecdc53b-01a7-5355-9761-f4d0cd7c4210" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186550039 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186550006 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186550044 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186550039 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186550044 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3aa6c554-176f-5c59-afc8-934d6bc78ac1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186550044 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186549989 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186550047 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186550087 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186550047 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3dc10566-71f1-5733-8f61-e93c9e40f5b1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186550047 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186069424 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186550056 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186550044 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186550056 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "56bb3c30-2afe-5b11-a004-62947497ce5e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186550056 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186550009 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186550062 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186550075 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186550062 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "5ed022d1-55d7-5557-9818-d5cd788aef99" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186550062 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186549950 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186550066 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186550062 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186550066 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "6da7d17